# Comments and Documentation Tone

Write comments and docs for **future engineers reading the product**, not for
an AI chat transcript. Tone is neutral, precise, and durable.

## Do

- Comment **why** (constraint, hazard, non-obvious tradeoff) — not what the
  next line obviously does.
- Prefer measured technical language: failure mode, units, bounds, protocol
  requirement, measured symptom.
- Keep README/API notes factual and scannable; put deep rationale in code
  comments or `docs/` when length warrants it.
- Match existing voice (e.g. RS485 turnaround / DMA priority notes in
  `uart5_rx.c`, `dma.c`, `audio_bridge.c`).

## Do not

- Conversational asides, scare-quotes for informal metaphors, emoji, or
  “chat with the assistant” phrasing (`we should`, `let's`, `as discussed`).
- Leaving investigation breadcrumbs in production text (`still broken?`,
  `tried X`, `TODO for the agent`, `FIXME later maybe`).
- Restating the code or padding docs with narrative that does not help a
  maintainer act.
- In **card firmware**, coupling comments to host/tool paths or binaries
  (`tools/gen_sample_waves.py`, `midi_bridge`, `cmi_control`) — see
  `firmware-card-purity.mdc`. Host tools may cite card constants; the card
  does not cite the tools.

## Examples

```text
BAD (chatty / informal):
  Integer Hz breaks equal-temperament octaves (C4→262 / C5→523 → ~1 Hz beat /
  “dip” when held together).

GOOD (production):
  Send frequencies as fractional ASCII, not integer Hz. Integer rounding
  detunes equal-temperament octaves (e.g. C4→262 Hz, C5→523 Hz instead of
  261.63 / 523.25) and produces ~1 Hz beating when those notes sound together.
```

```c
/* BAD — narrates the obvious */
/* Set the pin high */
HAL_GPIO_WritePin(...);

/* GOOD — records a non-obvious constraint */
/* Busy-wait: HAL_Delay deadlocks if called from USB IRQ context. */
```

When editing docs or comments you did not write, upgrade tone to match this
rule if you touch the surrounding prose; do not rewrite unrelated sections
for style alone.
